  "question": {
    "text": "A soft drink was bottled with a partial pressure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> of&nbsp;3 bar&nbsp;over the liquid at room temperature. The partial pressure of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> over the solution approaches a value of 30 bar when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>44</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is dissolved in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> of water at room temperature. The approximate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi></math> of the soft drink is ___________<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>.<br />(First dissociation constant of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>;</mo></math> density of the soft drink&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mL</mi><mrow><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>)"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "37",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Amount of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> in one liter of solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Mole</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>{</mo><mi>pKa</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>logC</mi><mo>}</mo></math></p><p>For a weak acid solution</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>{</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>}</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>7</mn></math></p><p>&nbsp;</p>"
  }
